## 1. What is it?

A custom pipe is a class that transforms a value for display in a template, using the `{{ value | pipeName:arg }}` syntax. "With arguments" means the pipe takes extra parameters beyond the value itself, like `timeAgo:'short'` or `highlight:searchTerm`. "Pure" is a setting on the pipe that controls exactly when Angular bothers to re-run it.

## 2. Why introduced?

Formatting logic like "turn a timestamp into '5 minutes ago'" or "wrap matching text in `<mark>`" shows up in templates all the time. Pipes let you write that logic once and reuse it declaratively in any template, instead of repeating formatting code in every component or computing it manually on every change. Purity was added on top of that so Angular doesn't have to blindly re-run every pipe's logic on every single change detection pass — for a pipe whose output depends only on its inputs, that would be wasted work.

## 3. How does it work?

A pipe is a class with a `transform(value, ...args)` method, decorated with `@Pipe({ name: '...' })`. By default, a pipe is **pure** (you don't even need to write `pure: true` — it's the default). A pure pipe only re-runs `transform()` when the bound value or any of its arguments changes, compared to the last call, using reference comparison for objects and value comparison for primitives. If nothing it's watching changed, Angular reuses the last output without calling `transform()` again — even if some totally unrelated part of the page just triggered a change detection pass.

Setting `pure: false` makes a pipe **impure**. An impure pipe re-runs `transform()` on every single change detection pass that reaches its component, no matter what actually changed — even if none of its own inputs did. This is expensive in a large list, but it's necessary for pipes whose output can change without any input reference changing (like a pipe that reads from a mutable array in place, without ever replacing the array itself).

## 4. Example

Verified live at `https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-lheyr15u`. A `timeAgo` pipe built twice — once pure, once impure — plus a pure `highlight` pipe that takes a search term. A shared counter service tracks exactly how many times each pipe's `transform()` actually runs.

```typescript
// pipe-call-counter.service.ts
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';

/**
 * Deliberately NOT signal-based: writing to a signal from inside a pipe's
 * transform() throws NG0600 ("Writing to signals is not allowed in a
 * computed or an effect"), because template refresh runs pipes inside a
 * reactive-context guard. A plain mutable object works fine here -- the
 * root component uses Default change detection, so its template
 * re-reads these plain properties on every tick regardless of how they
 * changed.
 */
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class PipeCallCounterService {
  counts = { pure: 0, impure: 0, highlight: 0 };

  incrementPure() {
    this.counts.pure++;
  }
  incrementImpure() {
    this.counts.impure++;
  }
  incrementHighlight() {
    this.counts.highlight++;
  }
}
```

```typescript
// time-ago.pipe.ts
import { Pipe, PipeTransform, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { PipeCallCounterService } from './pipe-call-counter.service';

/**
 * PURE pipe (the default -- no `pure: false` set). Angular only re-runs
 * transform() when the bound value OR any argument changes by reference
 * (objects) or value (primitives), compared against the last call. It does
 * NOT re-run just because a change detection pass happened somewhere else
 * on the page. This is why it's safe and efficient to use in large lists.
 */
@Pipe({ name: 'timeAgo', standalone: true })
export class TimeAgoPipe implements PipeTransform {
  private counter = inject(PipeCallCounterService);

  transform(value: number, unit: 'short' | 'long' = 'long'): string {
    this.counter.incrementPure();
    const seconds = Math.floor((Date.now() - value) / 1000);
    if (seconds < 60) return unit === 'short' ? `${seconds}s` : `${seconds} seconds ago`;
    const minutes = Math.floor(seconds / 60);
    if (minutes < 60) return unit === 'short' ? `${minutes}m` : `${minutes} minute${minutes === 1 ? '' : 's'} ago`;
    const hours = Math.floor(minutes / 60);
    return unit === 'short' ? `${hours}h` : `${hours} hour${hours === 1 ? '' : 's'} ago`;
  }
}
```

```typescript
// time-ago-impure.pipe.ts
import { Pipe, PipeTransform, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { PipeCallCounterService } from './pipe-call-counter.service';

/**
 * IMPURE pipe (pure: false). Angular re-runs transform() on EVERY change
 * detection pass, anywhere on the page -- regardless of whether the value
 * or arguments bound to this exact pipe usage changed at all. Identical
 * logic to TimeAgoPipe, kept as a separate class purely to compare call
 * counts side by side in the demo.
 */
@Pipe({ name: 'timeAgoImpure', standalone: true, pure: false })
export class TimeAgoImpurePipe implements PipeTransform {
  private counter = inject(PipeCallCounterService);

  transform(value: number, unit: 'short' | 'long' = 'long'): string {
    this.counter.incrementImpure();
    const seconds = Math.floor((Date.now() - value) / 1000);
    if (seconds < 60) return unit === 'short' ? `${seconds}s` : `${seconds} seconds ago`;
    const minutes = Math.floor(seconds / 60);
    if (minutes < 60) return unit === 'short' ? `${minutes}m` : `${minutes} minute${minutes === 1 ? '' : 's'} ago`;
    const hours = Math.floor(minutes / 60);
    return unit === 'short' ? `${hours}h` : `${hours} hour${hours === 1 ? '' : 's'} ago`;
  }
}
